WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:05.460
An important aspect of DNS to understand&nbsp;
both for the exam and beyond is the concept&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.460 --> 00:00:08.400
of different types of resources&nbsp;
records and what they do in DNS.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:08.940 --> 00:00:13.380
Here we’ll cover some of the more popular&nbsp;
types of resource records starting with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:13.380 --> 00:00:20.580
most common type of record an A record. A&nbsp;
records resolve host names to IP addresses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.300 --> 00:00:31.680
specifically IPv4 records. AAAA or quad A&nbsp;
records resolve host names to IPv6 addresses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:32.460 --> 00:00:40.260
where as A records resolve host names to IP&nbsp;
addresses. Pointer records (PTR) records do just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:48.780
the opposite, they resolve IP addresses to fully&nbsp;
qualified domain names a reverse of A records.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:51.720 --> 00:00:56.640
CNAME records canonical name records&nbsp;
create aliases for host names,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.300 --> 00:01:01.920
this allows users to access a machine by&nbsp;
a potentially easier to understand name&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.580 --> 00:01:09.360
rather than a possibly cryptic name for&nbsp;
the machine. And then we have MX records,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:15.000
MX Mail Exchanger records are used to&nbsp;
indicate mail servers used in a network,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:21.960
these and CNAME records must be entered&nbsp;
manually, an MX record has to entered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:21.960 --> 00:01:28.740
even after a mail server like Exchange is&nbsp;
installed. Start of authority (SOA) records&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:35.280
contain data for DNS zones, which are the parts&nbsp;
of a domain each DNS server is responsible for.

